# ── PostgreSQL ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two connections by design, for tenant-isolation integrity.
# Defaults below match the Docker quickstart in README.md (container port 5433,
# passwords from db/bootstrap.sql). Using a native Postgres? Change 5433 → 5432.
#
# DATABASE_URL — the APP connection. Connects as role `arena_app`, which is
#   NOT a superuser and does NOT have BYPASSRLS. Every tenant-facing query runs
#   through it inside a transaction that sets `app.user_id`, so Row-Level
#   Security is always enforced. This is what the running app uses.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://arena_app:arena_app_pw@localhost:5433/arena_os
#
# DATABASE_URL_OWNER — the OWNER connection. Connects as a privileged role that
#   owns the tables and BYPASSES RLS. Used ONLY for migrations, seeding and
#   platform provisioning (tenant signup, session/user lookups). Never used on a
#   tenant data path.
DATABASE_URL_OWNER=postgres://arena_owner:arena_owner_pw@localhost:5433/arena_os

# ── Auth ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Secret used to sign the session cookie. Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
SESSION_SECRET=change-me-to-a-long-random-string

# ── App ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Root domain tenants are subdomains of. In local dev use lvh.me:3000
# (lvh.me resolves *.lvh.me to 127.0.0.1, so subdomains work with no setup).
NEXT_PUBLIC_ROOT_DOMAIN=lvh.me:3000

# ── Object storage (S3-compatible) ─────────────────────────

S3_BUCKET=your-bucket-name
S3_REGION=auto
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=change-me
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=change-me
S3_ENDPOINT=your_s3_endpoint
S3_PUBLIC_URL_BASE=your_s3_endpoint/bucket_name

# ── Secret encryption ───────────────────────────────────────
# Master key for application-level encryption of secrets held at rest — today
# the per-tenant Razorpay key secret (lib/security/encryption.ts, AES-256-GCM).
#
# SERVER ONLY. Never prefix this with NEXT_PUBLIC_, never commit a real value,
# and never store it in the database. Without it, saving payment credentials
# fails with a configuration error — there is deliberately no fallback.
#
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
# Rotating it makes every previously stored secret undecryptable, so existing
# credentials must be re-entered. The ciphertext format is versioned (`v1:…`)
# so a future migration can re-wrap instead.
PAYMENT_SETTINGS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ── Customer OTP login (M9 / AROS-87) ───────────────────────
# Phone/OTP sign-in for the customer portal. Separate from the staff login
# above: different table, different cookie.

# OTP_DEV_BYPASS — LOCAL DEV ONLY. Off by default; uncomment to use it.
# When on, no SMS is sent and the code 123456 signs in ANY phone number — an
# open door, so never enable it on a shared, staging or preview box. (The app
# also refuses to start with it on under NODE_ENV=production.)

# OTP_DEV_BYPASS=true

# MSG91 — the SMS gateway used when the bypass is off. Required in production,
# not needed while the bypass is on. Server-only: never prefix NEXT_PUBLIC_.
MSG91_AUTH_KEY=
MSG91_TEMPLATE_ID=
# Optional 6-character DLT sender/header, e.g. ARENA. Blank if the template fixes it.
MSG91_SENDER_ID=
# Optional, default 5. lib/otp/challenge.ts holds the authoritative TTL.
MSG91_OTP_EXPIRY_MINUTES=5

# The OTP hash is keyed by SESSION_SECRET above (lib/otp/code.ts), so that must
# be a real random value for customer login to work at all.

# ── Platform subscription billing (Razorpay Subscriptions) ─────────────
# Arena OS charges BUSINESSES for their subscription through its OWN Razorpay
# merchant account. That account's credentials are NOT environment variables:
# they are entered by a platform admin at /admin/billing and stored encrypted
# in `platform_payment_settings` (migration 0051), the same way a tenant's own
# keys are stored in `payment_settings`.
#
# So there is nothing new to set here. What this billing path DOES require:
#
#   PAYMENT_SETTINGS_ENCRYPTION_KEY  (above) — the platform key secret and
#     webhook secret are sealed with this same master key, under the fixed AAD
#     'platform:razorpay'. A tenant's secrets use their tenant id as AAD, so the
#     two are cryptographically non-interchangeable. Without this key set,
#     saving platform gateway credentials fails with a configuration error.
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_ROOT_DOMAIN          (above) — the platform webhook lives on the
#     ROOT domain, not a tenant subdomain, because there is one platform
#     Razorpay account for the whole install:
#
#       https://${NEXT_PUBLIC_ROOT_DOMAIN}/api/webhooks/platform-razorpay
#
#     Register that URL on the Arena OS Razorpay account and subscribe it to the
#     `subscription.*` events. It is a DIFFERENT URL and a DIFFERENT account
#     from the per-venue deposit webhook at {slug}.${ROOT}/api/webhooks/razorpay.
#
# Operator setup, once per install:
#   0. /admin/billing  → invoice letterhead: Arena OS's legal name, GSTIN,
#      registered address, GST state code, GST rate and the invoice/credit-note
#      number prefixes. Required before any GST invoice can be raised, and
#      snapshotted onto every invoice so later edits never rewrite an old bill.
#   1. /admin/billing  → key ID, key secret, webhook signing secret.
#   2. Create the Subscription plans on the Razorpay dashboard — one per
#      Arena OS plan PER BILLING PERIOD (monthly and yearly are separate plan
#      objects because they are separate prices).
#   3. /admin/plans    → paste each `plan_…` into its Arena OS plan. The server
#      re-checks the gateway plan's price against the catalogue before creating
#      any subscription, so a transposed id is refused rather than mischarged.
#
# Note on prices: a plan's catalogue price is what Razorpay captures, so it is
# treated as GST-INCLUSIVE and the tax is extracted from it. Set the price you
# want the business actually charged.

# ── DEPLOYMENT REQUIREMENT: forwarded-IP headers ────────────────────────────
#
# /signup is the only PUBLIC, unauthenticated write endpoint in this app, and
# its per-IP rate limits (lib/actions/signup.ts) are keyed off the caller IP
# that lib/security/ip.ts reads from `X-Forwarded-For`, falling back to
# `X-Real-IP`. Those headers are only as trustworthy as the last hop that set
# them: if requests can reach the app WITHOUT passing through a proxy that
# OVERWRITES `X-Forwarded-For`, a client can spoof it and get a fresh rate-limit
# bucket per request.
#
# So before exposing /signup publicly, make sure the load balancer / ingress /
# CDN in front of this app overwrites (not appends to) `X-Forwarded-For`, and
# that the app is not reachable directly on its own port.
#
# The per-EMAIL cap (3/hour) is not IP-keyed and still holds regardless, which
# is why signup is not wide open even if the above is misconfigured — but it is
# the second line of defence, not the first.
#
# Note also that the limiter's counters live in an in-process Map, so every
# additional app instance gets its own allowance. With N instances the effective
# cap is N× the configured one. Fine for a single-instance deployment; move the
# counters to Redis (or equivalent) before scaling out.

# ── Google Business Profile (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────
# NOTHING GOES HERE. The OAuth client is configured PER TENANT, in Settings →
# Business profile, because quota and app verification are both per Google
# Cloud project: one shared platform client would split ~300 QPM across every
# venue and would need Google's harder multi-tenant review for the sensitive
# business.manage scope. Each venue brings its own project, its own quota and
# its own verification. See migration 0105.
